# session/exclude -- windows session never captures and never places.
#
# session records where your windows sit and puts them back on restart. A few
# windows must be exempt: transient states that should open wherever you
# currently are (a blank New Tab, the Chrome profile picker), not be dragged to
# a remembered spot. This file is that repository -- grow it as you meet more.
#
# One rule per line, always the same shape:
#
#   <app-regex> :: <title-regex>
#
# BOTH fields are required. Each is a Python regex matched with re.search, so
# it is UNANCHORED (a substring test) -- add ^...$ to pin it, exactly as the
# work boundary's own ^\[WORK does. Use .* for "any" in either field:
#
#   .* :: ^New Tab$          any app, whose title is exactly "New Tab"
#   ^google-chrome$ :: .*    every google-chrome window
#
# A window matching ANY rule here is never recorded and never moved. A line
# beginning with # is a comment; blank lines are ignored. `session exclude`
# prints the rules as loaded and flags any bad line. Edits apply AUTOMATICALLY:
# the running watcher re-reads this file within a second of saving.
#
# NOTE: the work/personal boundary (titles starting "[WORK") is NOT here -- it
# is a hardcoded safety wall inside session, not a tunable. Never rely on this
# file for it.

# Chrome transient states: blank tabs and the pre-load window title. A stored
# "New Tab" would drag every future new tab to one spot; a bare "Google Chrome"
# entry (worn briefly before a page title loads) would fight every window
# mid-load. Chrome sometimes appends " - Google Chrome", hence the optional
# suffix. Covers google-chrome and chromium.
google-chrome|chromium :: ^Google Chrome$
google-chrome|chromium :: ^New Tab( - Google Chrome)?$
google-chrome|chromium :: ^New Incognito Tab( - Google Chrome)?$
google-chrome|chromium :: ^Sign in
google-chrome|chromium :: ^Settings -.* Google Chrome$

org.gnome.Loupe :: ^.*$
